add(1496,'schematic-omission','Completing the schematic matrix diagram','The diagram says scaling and softmax are omitted; what operation is actually needed before multiplying the score table by V?', ['Scale scores, apply the causal mask, and take row-wise softmax','Multiply the −infinity masked scores directly by V','Apply softmax independently to each coordinate of V instead','Replace every negative score by its absolute value'], 'The weighted sum uses normalized finite attention weights, not raw masked logits.', ['Direct multiplication by −infinity logits is not the intended attention operation.','That normalizes the wrong quantity and axis.','Absolute values change score meaning and do not implement normalization.'],conflict='The schematic explicitly leaves out scaling and softmax; its masked score table is not itself the weight matrix.')
add(1496,'quadratic-table','Scaling the attention table','If sequence length grows from 100 to 200 while head widths stay fixed, how does the number of entries in a dense QKᵀ table change?', ['It grows from 10000 to 40000, a factor of 4','It grows from 10000 to 20000, a factor of 2','It remains 10000 because feature widths are fixed','It grows from 100 to 200, since the table is one-dimensional'], 'The dense table has N rows and N columns, so doubling N quadruples its entries.', ['This accounts for only one dimension.','The token dimensions grow even if feature widths do not.','The score table is two-dimensional.'],typ='numerical',steps=['100²=10000.','200²=40000.','40000/10000=4.'])
add(1497,'vectorized-heads','Head index versus token index','In the vectorized multi-head equation, what does headi contain for one head i?', ['An N×dv matrix of outputs for all N token positions','Only the output of token i from every head','A vocabulary-sized vector of sampled token IDs','A single scalar shared by all positions and values'], 'The head index selects a projection set; each such head processes all token rows.', ['This confuses the head index with a token index.','Head outputs are contextual features, not sampled IDs.','Each position has its own vector output.'])
add(1497,'identity-branches','Zero branch outputs in a pre-norm block','If both the attention and FFN branches return zero vectors, what is the block output in the displayed equations?', ['H=X','H=0','H=LayerNorm(X) regardless of the residuals','H=2X'], 'The two residual additions preserve X when neither branch adds an update.', ['This discards the residual path.','Normalization occurs inside branches, not as a final replacement of the stream.','The equations add branch outputs, not an extra copy of X at both stages.'],role='premise')
add(1498,'onehot-shape','One-hot sequence representation','For N=5 tokens and vocabulary size 300, what is the one-hot sequence matrix shape?', ['5×300','300×5','5×5','300×300'], 'There is one row per token and one column per vocabulary entry.', ['This reverses the row and column meanings.','This confuses vocabulary size with sequence length.','This incorrectly creates one row per vocabulary entry.'],typ='numerical',steps=['Use N=5 rows.','Use |V|=300 columns.'])
add(1498,'id-convention','Interpreting the displayed index example','The slide maps Janet to ID 5 and shows four zeros before the 1; which convention makes that illustration consistent?', ['One-based IDs, so ID 5 selects the fifth entry','Zero-based IDs, so ID 5 selects the fifth entry','IDs are the embedding values rather than entry positions','Every ID must map to the first entry'], 'Four preceding zeros place the one in the fifth entry, which matches a one-based convention.', ['A zero-based ID 5 selects the sixth entry.','IDs choose lookup entries; they are not the embedded feature values.','Different IDs select different entries.'],conflict='The illustrative ID 5 and one-hot vector imply one-based indexing; software tokenizers often use zero-based indexing.')
add(1498,'token-not-word','Word tokens in an illustrative sentence','What should be inferred from treating “Janet will back the bill” as five tokens on this slide?', ['It is the example’s word-level tokenization, not a rule that every tokenizer uses one token per word','Every language model must tokenize every word into exactly one token','The same sentence must have five tokens under every vocabulary','Punctuation and subword units can never be tokens'], 'The illustration fixes a simple tokenization for explaining the matrix shapes.', ['Tokenization can use subwords or other units.','The token count depends on the tokenizer and vocabulary.','Token vocabularies can include punctuation and subword units.'],assumptions=['No claim is made about a particular deployed model tokenizer.'])
add(1499,'lookup-example','Embedding lookup by one-hot multiplication','Let E=[[1,2],[3,4],[5,6]] and W=[[0,1,0],[1,0,0]]; what is WE?', ['[[3,4],[1,2]]','[[1,2],[3,4]]','[[4,6],[4,6]]','[[0,1,0],[1,0,0]]'], 'Each one-hot row selects its corresponding row from E.', ['This ignores the first row’s selection of the second vocabulary entry.','This adds embeddings rather than selecting one per token.','This leaves the one-hot IDs unembedded.'],typ='numerical',steps=['The first row selects E row 2: (3,4).','The second selects E row 1: (1,2).'])
add(1499,'position-add','Adding position representations','With Xword=[[3,4],[1,2]] and Epos=[[0.1,0.2],[0.3,0.4]], what is the composite input X?', ['[[3.1,4.2],[1.3,2.4]]','[[3,4,0.1,0.2],[1,2,0.3,0.4]]','[[0.3,0.8],[0.3,0.8]]','[[3.3,4.4],[1.1,2.2]]'], 'The slide adds matching word and position entries element by element.', ['This concatenates rather than adds the representations.','This multiplies entries rather than adding them.','This swaps the two position rows.'],typ='numerical',steps=['Add the first rows: (3,4)+(0.1,0.2).','Add the second rows: (1,2)+(0.3,0.4).'])
add(1499,'position-purpose','Why add position information?', ['Which purpose best matches the position-embedding step?'][0], ['Provide order information alongside token identity','Make every occurrence of every token have the same full input representation','Convert the vocabulary size into the batch size','Replace all attention weights with token IDs'], 'Position information distinguishes where tokens occur in the sequence.', ['Different positions can contribute different position representations.','Vocabulary size and batch size are unrelated axes.','The position representation does not replace attention weights.'])
add(1499,'position-variant','Learned absolute positions are one design','Which reading of the learned Epos matrix is appropriately limited?', ['It describes this design; Transformers can also use other positional schemes','Every Transformer must use a learned absolute-position table','A model with sinusoidal positions cannot have attention','The position matrix must always have exactly five rows for every input'], 'The slide illustrates learned absolute positions, not a universal requirement.', ['Other position representations exist.','Attention is compatible with fixed sinusoidal positions.','Five is the example sequence length rather than a universal length.'],conflict='The worked example uses learned absolute positions and a pre-trained lookup; these are design choices, not universal Transformer requirements.',primary=paper)
add(1500,'unembedding-shape','Language-model projection dimensions','For hidden width d=64 and vocabulary size 1000, what shape maps a 1×64 final row to vocabulary logits?', ['64×1000','1000×64','64×64','1000×1000'], 'The output projection needs 64 input rows and 1000 output columns.', ['This is the token-embedding orientation rather than its output-map transpose.','This keeps the hidden width instead of producing vocabulary scores.','This does not match the input hidden width.'],typ='numerical',steps=['The hidden row is 1×64.','Right-multiply by 64×1000.','The result is 1×1000.'])
add(1500,'weight-tying','Tied input and output weights','What does U=Eᵀ mean in the displayed language-model head?', ['The output projection shares the input-embedding weights by transposition','The vocabulary probability vector is identical to the token-ID list','Every Transformer architecture is required to use this tying','The output softmax has no role because E is already probabilities'], 'The shown design reuses E for the output map; tying is an architectural choice.', ['IDs are not a probability vector.','Untied output projections are also possible.','Embedding values are not already a vocabulary probability distribution.'],conflict='The slide presents tied Eᵀ as the head; this is the illustrated model choice rather than a requirement for all language models.',primary=paper)
add(1500,'logits','Logits versus probabilities','Which statement distinguishes logits u from probabilities y?', ['Logits are raw scores; softmax produces nonnegative entries summing to one','Logits must already be between zero and one and sum to one','Softmax preserves every logit unchanged','A negative logit makes a token probability negative'], 'Softmax maps unconstrained real scores to a normalized probability distribution.', ['Those constraints apply to probabilities, not general logits.','Exponentiation and normalization generally change the entries.','Exponentials are positive for finite logits.'])
add(1500,'lm-softmax','A small language-model head','For h=(1,0), E=[[0,1],[ln 2,0],[ln 3,1]] and U=Eᵀ, what vocabulary distribution follows?', ['(1/6,2/6,3/6)','(0,ln 2,ln 3)','(1/3,1/3,1/3)','(0,2/5,3/5)'], 'The logits are (0,ln 2,ln 3), so the exponentials are (1,2,3) and normalize by 6.', ['These are logits rather than probabilities.','The logits are not equal.','exp(0)=1, so the first token has nonzero probability.'],typ='numerical',diff='medium',steps=['Multiply h by Eᵀ to get (0,ln 2,ln 3).','Exponentiate to (1,2,3).','Divide by the total 6.'])
add(1501,'last-position','Which output predicts the next token?', ['For a prefix of N tokens, which hidden output is passed to the shown next-token head?'][0], ['The final-layer representation at position N','The raw ID of token 1 without any contextual processing','The future hidden state at position N+1 before that token exists','Only the average of all position IDs'], 'The diagram maps the final position’s contextual hidden vector to the distribution for the next token.', ['This omits the contextual stack and current prefix position.','The future token has not yet been selected or processed.','Position IDs alone are not contextual content.'],typ='diagram')
add(1501,'greedy','Greedy selection is not random sampling','With token probabilities A=0.6, B=0.3 and C=0.1, what does greedy decoding select?', ['A every time, assuming the same scores and no tie','A with probability 0.6 and B with probability 0.3','B because it is the median-probability token','A, B and C uniformly'], 'Greedy decoding takes the maximum-probability token rather than drawing randomly from the distribution.', ['This describes probabilistic sampling, not greedy selection.','Greedy selection does not choose the median.','Uniform selection ignores the model probabilities.'],conflict='The slide calls choosing the highest-probability token sampling; distinguish deterministic greedy selection from stochastic sampling.')
add(1502,'top-k-rule','Top-k candidate selection','What does top-k sampling retain before renormalizing?', ['The k highest-probability tokens','The k lowest-probability tokens','All tokens whose IDs are less than k','Exactly k percent of the vocabulary probability mass'], 'Top-k fixes the number of candidates by probability rank.', ['This reverses the ranking.','Vocabulary ID order is not probability order.','A fixed probability-mass threshold describes a different selection rule.'])
add(1502,'top-k-numeric','Top-k renormalization','For probabilities (0.5,0.3,0.15,0.05) and k=2, what is the retained distribution?', ['(0.625,0.375,0,0)','(0.5,0.3,0,0)','(0.5,0.5,0,0)','(0,0,0.75,0.25)'], 'The top two have total mass 0.8; divide each retained probability by 0.8.', ['The retained entries must be renormalized to sum to one.','Sampling preserves relative probabilities rather than making them equal.','This keeps the two least likely candidates.'],typ='numerical',steps=['Keep 0.5 and 0.3.','Their sum is 0.8.','Normalize to 0.625 and 0.375.'])
add(1502,'nucleus-rule','Nucleus candidate selection','Which rule matches top-p sampling for p in (0,1]?', ['Take the smallest descending-probability prefix whose cumulative mass is at least p','Take exactly p tokens regardless of their probabilities','Keep every token whose individual probability exceeds p','Take the least likely tokens until their mass exceeds p'], 'The cumulative threshold sets an adaptive-size set of high-probability candidates.', ['p is a probability threshold, not a token count.','The rule is cumulative rather than an individual-token cutoff.','The selected prefix begins with the most likely tokens.'])
add(1502,'nucleus-numeric','Nucleus calculation at a boundary','For probabilities (0.5,0.3,0.15,0.05) and p=0.8, what does the stated ≥ rule retain?', ['The first two tokens, normalized to (0.625,0.375)','Only the first token, with probability 1','The first three tokens because equality is insufficient','All four tokens because p means 80 tokens'], 'The first two sum to exactly 0.8 and satisfy the stated inclusive threshold.', ['The first token alone has mass 0.5, below the threshold.','The question explicitly uses ≥, so equality suffices.','p=0.8 means 80% probability mass, not a token count.'],typ='numerical',steps=['Cumulative mass after one token is 0.5.','After two it is 0.8, satisfying ≥0.8.','Normalize the retained pair by 0.8.'],assumptions=['Use exact arithmetic and the stated inclusive threshold; software boundary conventions must be checked separately.'])
add(1502,'nucleus-adaptation','Peaked versus flat distributions','At p=0.8, how many tokens are retained for distributions (0.85,0.1,0.05) and (0.4,0.35,0.25)?', ['One for the first and three for the second','One for both','Three for the first and one for the second','Two for both'], 'The first leading probability exceeds 0.8; the second needs all three because its top two sum to 0.75.', ['The second distribution needs more than one token.','This reverses the peaked and flatter cases.','Neither distribution crosses the threshold after exactly two in this example.'],typ='numerical',steps=['First cumulative mass starts at 0.85, so stop at one.','Second cumulative masses are 0.4,0.75,1.','It crosses 0.8 at three.'])
add(1502,'p-percent','Fraction versus percentage notation','In the displayed cumulative-mass inequality, which p represents a 90% threshold?', ['0.9','90','0.09','9'], 'The normalized probabilities sum to one, so 90% is written as the fraction 0.9.', ['Ninety exceeds the total available probability mass of one.','0.09 represents 9%.','Nine exceeds the total available probability mass.'],conflict='The prose says p percent, but the formula uses p as a fraction on the probability scale.',primary=nucleus)
add(1502,'truncation-not-uniform','Sampling after truncation','Once a top-k or top-p candidate set has been chosen, how are retained probabilities treated in the described method?', ['Divide by the retained total mass and sample from those relative probabilities','Assign identical probability to every retained token regardless of its original score','Keep the old probabilities without compensating for removed mass','Always choose the least likely retained token'], 'Renormalization preserves ratios among retained probabilities while making their sum one.', ['That would be uniform sampling over the set, a different method.','The probabilities would generally sum to less than one.','The sampling rule does not force the lowest retained candidate.'])
add(1503,'encoder-direction','Encoder attention direction','How does the shown encoder self-attention differ from causal decoder self-attention?', ['It can attend to both earlier and later positions in the available input sequence','It can access only the single immediately previous input token','It must hide all input positions after the first one','It predicts unknown future output tokens before receiving any input'], 'Removing the causal direction restriction permits bidirectional context over the known encoder input.', ['The encoder is not restricted to one previous token.','This is not the described encoder mask behavior.','Known input context differs from unknown future generated outputs.'],assumptions=['Padding or task-specific masks may still be used; unmasked here means no causal future-position restriction.'])
add(1503,'cross-attention','Reading the encoder-decoder connection','In encoder-decoder attention, where do the queries and the key/value memory come from?', ['Queries from the decoder state; keys and values from encoder outputs','Queries from future target labels; keys and values from unchosen token IDs','Queries and values only from the encoder, with no decoder state','All three only from a fixed vocabulary count'], 'The decoder uses its state to retrieve relevant information from the encoded source.', ['Future target labels are not available as unrestricted decoder inputs.','The decoder query specifies what source information is relevant to its current state.','A count is not a sequence of feature vectors.'],typ='diagram',role='premise',primary=paper)
add(1503,'decoder-three-sublayers','The original decoder stack','Which extra attention sublayer appears in the original encoder-decoder model’s decoder beyond masked self-attention?', ['Attention over the encoder output sequence','An attention layer that sees future target tokens without restriction','A layer that deletes the encoder representations before decoding','A layer that replaces all token vectors with their index numbers'], 'The original decoder includes cross-attention to the encoded input.', ['The target-side causal restriction remains.','The encoder outputs are used by the decoder.','Index numbers do not replace the contextual feature representations.'],typ='diagram')
add(1503,'vision-patches','Why encoder-style attention suits image patches','What benefit does the slide associate with bidirectional encoder processing in a Vision Transformer?', ['Each patch can use context from other available image patches without a left-to-right causal restriction','Every patch must wait for a newly generated word before it is processed','The image must be treated as a single scalar with no patch representations','All spatial relationships are automatically known without learning or position information'], 'The complete input image is available, so patch interactions need not follow a language-generation order.', ['This is not the described image-encoder computation.','ViT operates on visual token or patch representations.','Attention still learns relationships and needs an appropriate representation of positions.'])
